#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125421} :activity/id #uuid "95082e0a-322a-5587-9f1a-1280f50a5124" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049678 :topic/id #uuid "0a37136e-6c85-53df-835c-5cf2f76303d3" :topic/math-academy-id 1969} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125419 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125790 :tutorial/content "One useful property of diagonalization is that it allows us to easily determine the power of a matrix.\n\nAs an example, we consider the $2\\times 2$ matrix ![](images/53/53b7a9efd9f2ae1ef4adc7cee5f86f784722777b8c29f268dbd0e822ff1354f9.svg) with the diagonalization\n![](images/e3/e3111a91b80d238cf5255eb9a9884dcd3f80e914039c5e789e32c07b55cf5b06.svg)\n\nWe will use the diagonalization of $A$ to compute $A^{55}.$\n\nLet's start by computing $A^2.$ Using the properties of matrix multiplication, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =PD{P}^{-1}\\cdot PD{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =PD\\cancel{{P}^{-1}\\cdot P}D{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P(DD){P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}{P}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $A^3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot A \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}{P}^{-1}\\cdot PD{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}\\cancel{{P}^{-1}\\cdot P}D{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P({D}^{2}D){P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{3}{P}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix $P$ and its inverse $P^{-1}$ will always cancel each other out in the middle of the multiplication! So by induction, we have that\n\n$$\nA^{\\color{blue}55}=P \\cdot D^{\\color{blue}55} \\cdot P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, we only need to find $D^{\\color{blue}55}.$\n\nSince $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {D}^{3} & ={D}^{2}D=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFollowing this pattern, we get\n\n![](images/05/05e10fdc6944077a48b0214a41fae8d418a1b66c8d87a47d652158c84285c246.svg)\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{55} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{55}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given that $A = PDP^{-1},$ for any non-negative integer ${\\color{blue}k},$ we obtain \n\n$$\nA^{\\color{blue}k} = P \\cdot D^{\\color{blue}k} \\cdot P^{-1}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b7628bf0-02d4-514e-a564-669610253cd2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3576 :tutorial/title "Raising a Diagonalizable Matrix to a Power"} :step/id #uuid "934d1edd-bf1c-5c1f-8924-ede352d7d23b" :step/math-academy-id 20121 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125420}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125420 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124530 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124339 :question/id #uuid "dfb93cc8-6bf0-59d4-8fbc-c94f5c0c1a69" :question/math-academy-id "e-5502"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050446}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394724}] :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"} :step/id #uuid "d76255af-54d9-5dcb-9a9c-7b30b6c4a98a" :step/math-academy-id 20122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125422}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125421 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125791 :tutorial/content "Remember that the diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ and corresponding linearly independent eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ is\n![](images/cd/cde63e04857414720bed0cb247e1e0c4bae76b69823f4aeb494cda13c139ac13.svg)\n\nThis idea also works in reverse. Given a diagonalization $A$ in the form \n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe can immediately deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are the diagonal entries of $D,$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of $P.$ This is because the diagonalization of a matrix is (almost) unique.\n\n**Note:** The diagonalization is \"almost\" unique because the order of the eigenvalue-eigenvector pairs may vary, and the eigenvectors may be replaced with scalar multiples of themselves. However, none of these variations change the actual outcome regarding the eigenvalue-eigenvector pairs that we deduce." :tutorial/id #uuid "d23e5c1b-9483-574b-ab9f-f68b126993cd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3577 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6ed440e4-0b21-56bd-92c6-0ef75f28a68c" :step/math-academy-id 20123 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125492}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125422 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124340 :question/id #uuid "737021ad-5198-564f-bbe2-cc5a06ee5a82" :question/math-academy-id "e-5503"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427864}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"} :step/id #uuid "476486fe-26df-57a3-89ab-80eeb17483b7" :step/math-academy-id 20124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124381 :question/id #uuid "c1ebc3ac-7a26-565f-9798-9de531dc6e47" :question/math-academy-id "e-7133"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049113}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488274}] :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"} :step/id #uuid "1265d446-04a7-5d76-a5b8-e7c56bb1b4c0" :step/math-academy-id 26234 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125419}}] :activity/title "Properties of Diagonalization" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124754}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492090} :activity/id #uuid "3a218002-61a4-554f-ad7b-1fff0bdc3316" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049678 :topic/id #uuid "0a37136e-6c85-53df-835c-5cf2f76303d3" :topic/math-academy-id 1969} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0838f7e1-1e2a-5de4-899d-0534f10f1b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351641} {:answer/id #uuid "4bdae80f-03b9-55c3-aadd-11684fd45d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352262} {:answer/id #uuid "836126d0-86c5-54f1-8f69-77f8c1c3afc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352716} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a16fcf-857f-5b60-adf6-cb3cf7586045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353550} {:answer/id #uuid "f648b42e-29eb-56ba-be95-7cf583a892a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353717}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351641} :answer-field/id #uuid "29f200b4-abd8-556b-96a6-d61eebe5f500" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "18c9e3a0-322a-5b85-8d72-26e41db87e30" :question/math-academy-id "q-86682" :question/problem "Find ${A}^{71},$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A:$\n\n$$\nA={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{71} & =P{D}^{71}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{71}(\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)^{71} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{71}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1ba7f498-1881-5ab1-ad8c-dead16debd6e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492103}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75e25684-50a9-57d5-a453-f3280d5e984e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322066} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8f40aa-17fd-543a-b848-7fa918e8d3e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322287} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b7aa57-385e-526d-af9f-e30495eb3d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322558} {:answer/id #uuid "c02288ab-4da1-566a-bfba-eadf1b8d9b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322639} {:answer/id #uuid "e7df6b33-d577-517b-8051-21b8cf6af137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322066} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c700357-79a1-56bc-aa05-cd5a7a8ba8ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55fe01d2-18d3-58de-b08f-22bbfece1371" :question/math-academy-id "q-37362" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $N$ given that \n\n$$\nN\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $N:$\n\n$$\nN={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $N$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $N$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$"} :step/id #uuid "3bdc3345-f3fe-56c7-9d48-45ed67552ad2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c36a09-640e-5f2f-bee6-5e3ec7470874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 9 \\\\ -24 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269293} {:answer/id #uuid "3064e2aa-2353-5c23-8c5a-c90a2e00dae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 9 \\\\ -21 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269705} {:answer/id #uuid "47e19908-9201-5477-8a4f-5d2e1bcb5a23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269921} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb22d41-12a9-5433-9689-c05376bd7c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 6 \\\\ -12 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270252} {:answer/id #uuid "918b8499-30b8-5523-aa6d-ee8503603d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 6 \\\\ -18 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270536}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186270252} :answer-field/id #uuid "68aed225-1b65-5210-aa67-e6df22bc4d41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a83d9fbf-2f37-5a9f-b9ba-27fe8d23afc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-140132" :question/problem "Find ${C}^{36},$ where\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[18]{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[36]{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $C:$\n\n$$\nC={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[18]{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[36]{3}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $C=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${C}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{C}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{36} & =P{D}^{36}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[18]{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[36]{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{36}(\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}(\\sqrt[18]{3})^{36} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[36]{3})^{36}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {3}^{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}27 & -3 \\\\ -27 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 6 \\\\ -12 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "702b377c-b5a4-541e-aae2-92f1f1c6521f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123147cc-29a9-5a9b-8197-5141287824db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351722} {:answer/id #uuid "13590b66-311d-5d93-824a-b8885d95a619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351733} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce46df1-f81c-53bf-9562-397b90e58c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352387} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e2a4f2-1115-53ef-a5a2-b66e4951afd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353452} {:answer/id #uuid "e2dc21a8-005f-55af-bc53-974ee66b05b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353452} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece55d7d-c7d4-5233-ba8f-7f279a6be42b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5dc95dbe-f9a5-583f-bd3e-71e914c88035" :question/math-academy-id "q-86694" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}T=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $T:$\n\n$$\nT={{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $T$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$"} :step/id #uuid "74909bc5-d999-58f8-826a-ad660fd937ab" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492095}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492103 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121c30d3-381d-5eec-812d-224e51ec2ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351718} {:answer/id #uuid "28b0ed5c-9277-577c-b0f4-46d45ce08e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351944} {:answer/id #uuid "82f7010b-3ded-54da-922f-5d2a3139d0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352711} {:answer/id #uuid "b584defc-90ec-51d9-af5c-7473d8c554a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353119} {:answer/id #uuid "c68fd66d-ff23-5aca-a7e5-22e416ac2158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351718} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d0c1c4-db7a-5402-823c-10bcfe18c23c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a0ebddfd-78f1-5dda-8990-10394fcd7459" :question/math-academy-id "q-86693" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$"} :step/id #uuid "a769baed-2a11-58f0-9d78-7ebc9197c7d0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492096}}] :activity/title "Properties of Diagonalization" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491442}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513009} :activity/id #uuid "27c2cc0b-6344-5902-b1fc-5d7f76d70d6a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049678 :topic/id #uuid "0a37136e-6c85-53df-835c-5cf2f76303d3" :topic/math-academy-id 1969} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513005 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392121 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99904e20-cf2b-59e7-b362-654e7a4f8d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392123} {:answer/id #uuid "b220e044-2814-5fa8-8242-f5a49a8f9977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392124} {:answer/id #uuid "552b06a4-b57b-5fe3-86ff-f0c3f7777811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392125} {:answer/id #uuid "397fa46f-a0aa-5014-b020-3ff9307338ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 15 \\\\ 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392126} {:answer/id #uuid "391e7711-d133-5904-84eb-2912d457701e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392127}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392127} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f6152a7-a6df-58be-b360-b25866cf20a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "770a3bd2-95ed-564f-acba-14bf194a130c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37603" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A$, then  $A$ must be diagonalizable. \n\nSo, we can write $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $A$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nA={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 15 \\\\ 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "94c832ad-7f6f-569d-b0a4-050bc58efcae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513024}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0160f5a3-2ea1-5858-b954-3348e5f89bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321162} {:answer/id #uuid "02754f47-bdf7-52a7-926f-faa704f8c1c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321171} {:answer/id #uuid "58f54d69-961e-58e6-8bf8-7c203ba49701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321847} {:answer/id #uuid "6f0a589e-53b0-51b6-a7aa-ab1776d86608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322006} {:answer/id #uuid "b6eff625-fb6f-5013-b9cc-cca3da5bfac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322571}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322571} :answer-field/id #uuid "5700a7c9-a32d-554d-943b-5562d53a985c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "97605f63-4e2b-5abe-a9b2-c8a3d46a4bac" :question/math-academy-id "q-37271" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nC={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}(-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 15 \\\\ 20 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9e1f3daa-4674-5726-9801-55cbf66f4728" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513005}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513023 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce48fd3-6d90-5867-bc8c-48f781a9a134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351673} {:answer/id #uuid "6dea34ce-51cc-54ce-bd9e-2e2c5ec68b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352535} {:answer/id #uuid "71ac80d5-5537-5ad5-953b-dc791c6b3dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352561} {:answer/id #uuid "7239e333-3699-5e0e-a679-3638ec2f4a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -12 \\\\ 21 & -32\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352567} {:answer/id #uuid "7b23a21b-03eb-5d9a-8c5d-1e2dbf0ce327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -19 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351673} :answer-field/id #uuid "60ed20fe-9723-5a36-b174-23a11d61985b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a1fcc8b2-84cd-574b-a993-68fbd5d3c967" :question/math-academy-id "q-86680" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}(\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -15 \\\\ 12 & -30\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dd8b30df-d376-5fc5-94a5-8a3802e54cb9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513024 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351262 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "768e3afb-bb09-5e8d-afce-24265c595101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349537} {:answer/id #uuid "8b60cf77-97aa-5202-8d4e-811565f0e808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349708} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea6050c-f1c9-5dd1-b2f0-9331f4c3eb90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349731} {:answer/id #uuid "dd99276d-af91-5354-a572-a9a70ed58f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350384} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e335fc-583e-509e-ace9-504cc6b7c779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350384} :answer-field/id #uuid "91c9727d-1213-5c42-9fe0-34caddae9641" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c93e7214-d507-511f-96e2-a29d77beb762" :question/math-academy-id "q-81210" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}(\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 12 \\\\ 16 & 24\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e9899580-b410-524f-9c40-c83ad8446596" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513028}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513028 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0065af71-29a6-5ebd-9bb2-e8f6fd53309c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & 30 \\\\ -9 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351566} {:answer/id #uuid "0e38c9a8-52aa-57b5-8cd2-d15086786ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351684} {:answer/id #uuid "20bda32c-1ae4-5791-b78f-9ded45871b93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 17\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351857} {:answer/id #uuid "43179f12-d2ec-5fea-8ae8-1b1e8c829d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352184} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7a0b89-b520-516a-984b-548f08bea2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/re